---
title: Quickstart
description: Go from an empty route to a validated, accessible form in four steps — schema, server, fields, page.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

This walkthrough builds a settings form end to end. Every step is complete and runnable; together they are the smallest working Formsnap setup.

<Callout type="note">

Formsnap owns the markup and accessibility wiring. [Superforms](https://superforms.rocks) owns the data: loading, validation and submission stay exactly as Superforms documents them.

</Callout>

## 1. Define a schema

Any Superforms adapter works; this guide uses zod. Under Superforms 2.31+, import from `zod/v3` so the adapter's types match.

```ts title="src/routes/settings/schema.ts"
import { z } from "zod/v3";

export const settingsSchema = z.object({
	email: z.string().email("Enter a valid email address"),
	bio: z.string().max(250, "Keep it under 250 characters"),
});

export type SettingsData = z.infer<typeof settingsSchema>;
```

## 2. Load and validate on the server

`superValidate` creates the form in `load` and re-validates it in the action. Nothing here is Formsnap-specific.

```ts title="src/routes/settings/+page.server.ts"
import { fail } from "@sveltejs/kit";
import { superValidate } from "sveltekit-superforms";
import { zod } from "sveltekit-superforms/adapters";
import { settingsSchema } from "./schema.js";

export const load = async () => {
	return { form: await superValidate(zod(settingsSchema)) };
};

export const actions = {
	default: async ({ request }) => {
		const form = await superValidate(request, zod(settingsSchema));
		if (!form.valid) return fail(400, { form });
		return { form };
	},
};
```

## 3. Render the fields

This is where Formsnap takes over. A `Field` connects one schema path; its `Control` hands you props with the correct `id`, `name`, `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby` already set.

```svelte title="src/routes/settings/settings-form.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Field, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { SettingsData } from "./schema.js";

	let { validated }: { validated: SuperValidated<SettingsData> } = $props();

	// superForm reads the initial data once; the read is deliberately untracked.
	const form = superForm(untrack(() => validated));
	const { form: formData } = form;
</script>

<form method="POST">
	<Field {form} name="email" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Email</Label>
				<input type="email" {...props} bind:value={$formData.email} />
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}We'll email you about your account.{/snippet}
	</Field>

	<Field {form} name="bio" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Bio</Label>
				<textarea {...props} bind:value={$formData.bio}></textarea>
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}Tell us about yourself.{/snippet}
	</Field>

	<button type="submit">Save settings</button>
</form>
```

What you get without writing a line of wiring:

- `Label` is associated with the input via `for`/`id`.
- The `description` snippet renders a container the input references through `aria-describedby`.
- `fieldErrors` renders an error list that appears — and is announced — only when validation fails.

## 4. Pass the validated form to it

```svelte title="src/routes/settings/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import SettingsForm from "./settings-form.svelte";

	let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SettingsForm validated={data.form} />
```

Submit the form empty and the schema messages render next to their fields — no JavaScript required. Add `use:enhance` from Superforms when you want SPA-style submission; see [Progressive enhancement](/docs/guides/enhancement).

<Callout type="tip">

The snippets are optional. Omit `description` and no container is rendered; set `fieldErrors={false}` to place `<FieldErrors>` yourself anywhere inside the field.

</Callout>
